---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="LandingBio | Dan Ryan Neff"
  description="LandingBio explores digital identity, personal websites, and creating a place online that reflects who you are."
>
  <main>
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-sky-50 via-white to-indigo-50 dark:from-gray-900 dark:via-gray-900 dark:to-gray-800">
      <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl items-center gap-12 px-6 py-20 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:py-28">
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
            Project
          </p>

          <h1 class="mt-3 text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-6xl">
            LandingBio
          </h1>

          <p class="mt-6 text-2xl leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            A home for your digital identity.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
            LandingBio explores a simple idea: everyone deserves a place online
            that reflects who they are—not just another profile inside someone
            else’s platform.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
            <a
              href="https://landingbio.com/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg bg-sky-600 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-sky-700"
            >
              Visit LandingBio
              <span class="ml-2" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
            </a>

            <a
              href="/projects/"
              class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-gray-900 transition hover:bg-white dark:border-gray-700 dark:text-white dark:hover:bg-gray-800"
            >
              Back to Projects
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <img
            src="/images/dot-compilation.webp"
            alt="An abstract composition of connected points and forms"
            class="w-full rounded-2xl shadow-xl"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Idea -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
      <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
        The idea
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
        A place of your own on the web
      </h2>

      <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <p>
          Most people spend years building a digital presence, yet much of it
          exists on platforms they do not control. Social networks change.
          Algorithms change. Features disappear. Accounts come and go.
        </p>

        <p>
          A personal website offers something different. It can become a place
          where your work, interests, relationships, and identity develop over
          time without depending entirely on someone else’s priorities.
        </p>

        <p>
          LandingBio began as an exploration of how that kind of digital home
          could be easier to create while still encouraging thoughtful
          self-expression.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Beyond Link in Bio -->
    <section class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
        <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
          Beyond a list of links
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
          Digital presence can be more intentional
        </h2>

        <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
          A useful personal page does more than gather destinations. It helps a
          visitor understand the person behind them.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
          <article class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-8 shadow-sm dark:bg-gray-800">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
              Clarity
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Present yourself intentionally rather than chasing attention.
              Visitors should be able to understand what matters to you.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-8 shadow-sm dark:bg-gray-800">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
              Ownership
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Your identity should not depend entirely on a platform’s
              interface, algorithm, or business decisions.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-8 shadow-sm dark:bg-gray-800">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
              Simplicity
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              A focused page can communicate more clearly than a long
              collection of disconnected profiles and links.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="rounded-2xl bg-white p-8 shadow-sm dark:bg-gray-800">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
              Connection
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
              Technology can help people discover meaningful relationships,
              conversations, ideas, and opportunities.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Digital identity -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-20">
      <div class="grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
            Digital identity
          </p>

          <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
            A person is more than a profile
          </h2>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <p>
            A résumé emphasizes employment. A social profile emphasizes recent
            activity. A personal website can hold a much broader picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional work can exist alongside creative projects, learning,
            writing, photography, community involvement, personal interests, or
            new directions that have not yet become careers.
          </p>

          <p>
            That flexibility is part of what makes the web valuable. It gives
            people room to represent themselves more fully.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Evolving identity -->
    <section class="border-y border-gray-200 bg-white dark:border-gray-800 dark:bg-gray-950">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
          Digital identity is never finished
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <p>
            People change. Their work changes. Their interests deepen, separate,
            reconnect, and move in directions they could not have anticipated.
          </p>

          <p>
            A personal website can evolve alongside that process. It does not
            have to compress a person into a fixed professional label or a
            sequence of temporary posts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Over time, it can become a record of attention: what someone has
            built, explored, learned, valued, and chosen to share.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Technology and capability -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <div class="rounded-3xl bg-sky-950 px-8 py-14 text-white md:px-14">
        <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-300">
          Technology and capability
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-3xl font-bold md:text-4xl">
          Tools should expand expression, not flatten it
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-sky-100">
          <p>
            Digital tools often ask people to fit themselves into predefined
            categories, templates, and patterns of behavior.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those structures can be useful, but they can also narrow the range
            of what people are able to express. LandingBio explores how a
            structured tool might still leave room for personality, context,
            development, and choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The larger question is not only whether technology makes something
            easier. It is whether the technology helps people become more
            capable of presenting themselves with honesty and clarity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why I built it -->
    <section class="bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-900">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20">
        <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
          Why I built it
        </p>

        <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
          A small contribution to a more human web
        </h2>

        <div class="mt-8 space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          <p>
            LandingBio reflects a broader question that appears throughout my
            work:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-sky-500 pl-6 text-2xl font-medium leading-relaxed text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
            How can technology help people express who they are instead of
            continually asking them to become someone else?
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            I believe the web is still one of the best places for thoughtful
            self-expression. Personal websites create room for depth,
            curiosity, continuity, and ownership in ways that fast-moving
            social platforms often do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            LandingBio is one experiment in making that possibility more
            approachable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Closing -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-20 text-center">
      <p class="font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400">
        Explore the project
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white md:text-4xl">
        Visit LandingBio
      </h2>

      <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
        See the project itself, or return to the collection of projects and
        continue exploring.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <a
          href="https://landingbio.com/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg bg-sky-600 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-sky-700"
        >
          Visit LandingBio
          <span class="ml-2" aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>

        <a
          href="/projects/"
          class="inline-flex items-center rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-6 py-3 font-semibold text-gray-900 transition hover:bg-gray-50 dark:border-gray-700 dark:text-white dark:hover:bg-gray-800"
        >
          Back to Projects
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>